{"schema_version":"1.9.0","id":"OESA-2026-4411","published":"2026-10-22T14:34:50Z","modified":"2026-10-22T14:45:04.187114730Z","upstream":["CVE-2026-109618","CVE-2026-109619"],"summary":"php security update","details":"PHP is an HTML-embedded scripting language. PHP attempts to make it easy for developers to write dynamically generated web pages. PHP also offers built-in database integration for several commercial and non-commercial database management systems, so writing a database-enabled webpage with PHP is fairly simple. The most common use of PHP coding is probably as a replacement for CGI scripts. The php package contains the module (often referred to as mod_php) which adds support for the PHP language to Apache HTTP Server.\r\n\r\nSecurity Fix(es):\n\nPHP uses longjmp() for out-of-memory (OOM) errors to exit the current context and abort handling of the request. Some contexts may not properly account for this scenario and leave request memory in an uninitialized state before performing an allocation. The subsequent request cleanup procedure may read or branch on this memory. Multiple OOM strategies can consistently trigger this condition and lead to remote code execution (RCE).(CVE-2026-109618)\n\nPHP has various global state that temporarily points into request memory. In some scenarios, global state may not be correctly reset, or may be repopulated after being reset. The global state may then refer to request memory even after it has been freed. Subsequent requests may read or branch on this stale state before it is fully initialized. Multiple strategies can trigger this condition and produce remote code execution (RCE).(CVE-2026-109619)","affected":[{"package":{"name":"php","ecosystem":"openEuler:24.03-LTS-SP1","purl":"pkg:rpm/openEuler/php&distro=openEuler-24.03-LTS-SP1"},"ranges":[{"type":"ECOSYSTEM","events":[{"introduced":"0"},{"fixed":"8.3.36-1.oe2403sp1"}]}],"ecosystem_specific":{"aarch64":["php-8.3.36-1.oe2403sp1.aarch64.rpm","php-bcmath-8.3.36-1.oe